# `qp::Result`

What a quadratic-program solve returns: the solution, the objective, the multipliers, the residuals and whether it converged.

`Result` carries the primal solution `x`, the optimal `objective` value, the constraint values `z = A x` and the multipliers `y`, plus two scalar residuals, the iteration count and a `converged` flag. For a batch, `x`, `z`, `y` and `objective` have the batch dimensions in front. A `Result` is a plain value: gradients flow through its series when the inputs were gradient-tracked.

The multipliers `y` read like this: an entry is zero for a constraint that is not binding; positive where the upper bound is active, negative where the lower bound is active, and for an equality its sign says which direction the constraint is pulling. Always check `converged`: a solve that hits `max_iters` returns its current iterate with `converged = false`.

The method `warm()` returns a [`WarmStart`](WarmStart.md) built from `x`, `z` and `y`, to start a related solve from this solution.

## Fields

### `x`

`pluss::Series`, default ``.

The solution; batch dimensions lead.

### `objective`

`pluss::Series`, default ``.

The objective value `½ xᵀPx + qᵀx` at the solution, one per problem.

### `z`

`pluss::Series`, default ``.

The constraint values `A x` at the solution.

### `y`

`pluss::Series`, default ``.

The multipliers of the constraints.

### `primal_residual`

`double`, default `0`.

The size of the constraint violation at the final iterate.

### `dual_residual`

`double`, default `0`.

The size of the stationarity violation at the final iterate.

### `iterations`

`std::int64_t`, default `0`.

How many iterations were performed.

### `converged`

`bool`, default `false`.

Whether the residuals met the tolerance before the iteration cap.

## Guidance

Check `converged` before using `x`.

## Practical Examples

### Example 1

Reading every field of a solve. The equality has a large positive multiplier, the active lower bound on `x₀` a negative one, and the slack constraints zero.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

#include <limits>

using namespace pluss::tils;

namespace qp = pluss::subsp::math::qp;
using pluss::Series;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(5));
  // minimise x0^2 + x1^2 - 2 x0 - 5 x1 = 0.5 x'Px + q'x   subject to   x0 + x1 = 1,  0 <= x0 <= 0.7,  x1 >= 0
  const double inf = std::numeric_limits<double>::infinity();
  const Series P = pluss::series::make({{2.0, 0.0}, {0.0, 2.0}});
  const Series q = pluss::series::make({-2.0, -5.0});
  const Series A = pluss::series::make({{1.0, 1.0}, {1.0, 0.0}, {0.0, 1.0}});
  const Series lower = pluss::series::make({1.0, 0.0, 0.0});
  const Series upper = pluss::series::make({1.0, 0.7, inf});

  const qp::Result result = qp::solve(P, q, A, lower, upper);

  out.writeln("x         =", result.x.items<double>());
  out.writeln("objective =", result.objective.item<double>());
  out.writeln("z = A x   =", result.z.items<double>());
  out.writeln("y         =", result.y.items<double>());
  out.writeln("converged =", result.converged, ", iterations =", result.iterations);
  out.writeln("primal residual =", result.primal_residual, ", dual residual =", result.dual_residual);
}
```

### Example 2

`warm()`. It packages `x`, `z` and `y` as a `WarmStart`; the members are the result's own series.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

#include <limits>

using namespace pluss::tils;

namespace qp = pluss::subsp::math::qp;
using pluss::Series;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(6));
  // minimise x0^2 + x1^2 - 2 x0 - 5 x1 = 0.5 x'Px + q'x   subject to   x0 + x1 = 1,  0 <= x0 <= 0.7,  x1 >= 0
  const double inf = std::numeric_limits<double>::infinity();
  const Series P = pluss::series::make({{2.0, 0.0}, {0.0, 2.0}});
  const Series q = pluss::series::make({-2.0, -5.0});
  const Series A = pluss::series::make({{1.0, 1.0}, {1.0, 0.0}, {0.0, 1.0}});
  const Series lower = pluss::series::make({1.0, 0.0, 0.0});
  const Series upper = pluss::series::make({1.0, 0.7, inf});

  const qp::Result result = qp::solve(P, q, A, lower, upper);
  const qp::WarmStart start = result.warm();
  out.writeln("warm.x equals x :", (start.x - result.x).abs().max().item<double>() == 0.0);
  out.writeln("warm.y equals y :", (start.y - result.y).abs().max().item<double>() == 0.0);
}
```

### Example 3

`converged = false`. A capped run still returns an iterate, which is not a solution; the flag and the residuals say so.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

#include <limits>

using namespace pluss::tils;

namespace qp = pluss::subsp::math::qp;
using pluss::Series;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(6));
  // minimise x0^2 + x1^2 - 2 x0 - 5 x1 = 0.5 x'Px + q'x   subject to   x0 + x1 = 1,  0 <= x0 <= 0.7,  x1 >= 0
  const double inf = std::numeric_limits<double>::infinity();
  const Series P = pluss::series::make({{2.0, 0.0}, {0.0, 2.0}});
  const Series q = pluss::series::make({-2.0, -5.0});
  const Series A = pluss::series::make({{1.0, 1.0}, {1.0, 0.0}, {0.0, 1.0}});
  const Series lower = pluss::series::make({1.0, 0.0, 0.0});
  const Series upper = pluss::series::make({1.0, 0.7, inf});

  qp::Options capped;
  capped.max_iters = 5;
  const qp::Result result = qp::solve(P, q, A, lower, upper, capped);
  out.writeln("converged =", result.converged, ", iterations =", result.iterations);
  out.writeln("primal residual =", result.primal_residual, "(compare 5e-6 for a converged run)");
}
```

